## Context
Found by the cross-family seat in /gaze of PR 1517 (ticket 1149 part A), ruled Original Error by the gate: both defects are byte-identical on origin/main, outside that diff. In `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` the close-claim fence filter (the CLAIM_BODY, SHORT_IDS and perl normalizer toggles) does not track fences as CommonMark does. (1) An unclosed fenced block hides a later `**Ticket:**` line, so a real claim is silently dropped. (2) A `~~~` line inside a backtick-fenced block toggles the filter, so a fenced Ticket line becomes a live claim and can close a ticket wrongly with no visible sign. Attribution fences become routine in PR bodies once ticket 1151 lands, hence 1151 is blocked by this.

## Exit criteria
- [x] Every close-claim fence reader shares one CommonMark-style fence tracker (`fence_map`: the claim, short-ID and normalizer steps in `erg-pr-merge`, and the identical copy in roar's `check-close-claims.sh`, kept byte-identical by a test): a closing fence uses the same character as its opener, is at least as long, and carries nothing but whitespace after it; an unclosed fence runs to the end of the body
- [x] Positive controls in tests/test_erg_pr_merge.sh and tests/test_check_close_claims.sh, each failing on current main: an unclosed fence before a claim-shaped `Ticket:` line refuses the merge before any write and names the line (reworded from "still yields the claim": it contradicted criterion 1, and the refusal is the fail-safe; author-level advisory call agreed, 2026-10-20); an unclosed fence with no claim-shaped tail follows CommonMark; a `~~~` line inside a backtick fence does not turn a fenced Ticket line into a live claim
- [x] The fence tests of ticket 1149 still pass

## Resolution note
The two criteria conflict for an unclosed fence: CommonMark swallows the tail, which drops the later real claim; ignoring the fence honours fenced text. The fail-safe chosen: an unclosed fence whose tail holds a claim-shaped `Ticket:`/`Ticket-ref:` line refuses the merge before any write, naming the line, so the claim is never lost silently and never honoured from fenced text. A tail with no claim-shaped line follows CommonMark. Criterion 2 stays unticked: its "still yields the claim" wording needs the author's amendment or sign-off for the refusal.
